To lock two mutexes correctly, write <code>std::scoped_lock g(a, b);</code>. It acquires both through {{c1::std::lock}}'s deadlock-avoidance algorithm, so {{c2::the order of the arguments}} cannot deadlock even when another thread passes them in the opposite order. A documented {{c3::global lock order}} is the manual alternative.<pre><code>#include &lt;chrono&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;mutex&gt;
#include &lt;thread&gt;
std::mutex a, b;
int main() {
    std::thread t1([] {
        std::scoped_lock g(a, b);
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(50));
    });
    std::thread t2([] {
        std::scoped_lock g(b, a);
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(50));
    });
    t1.join();
    t2.join();
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "both threads finished, no deadlock\n";
}</code></pre><br><b>Real output:</b><pre><code>both threads finished, no deadlock</code></pre>

The destructor of a future returned by <code>std::async(std::launch::async, f)</code> {{c1::blocks until the task finishes}}. A discarded async call therefore makes the statement {{c2::synchronous}}, which is a common way to serialise by accident.<pre><code>#include &lt;chrono&gt;
#include &lt;future&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;thread&gt;
int slow() {
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(100));
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "slow body finished\n";
    return 1;
}
int main() {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "launching\n";
    std::async(std::launch::async, slow);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "after async statement\n";
}</code></pre><br><b>Real output:</b><pre><code>launching
slow body finished
after async statement</code></pre>

A {{c1::std::jthread}} requests stop and then joins in its destructor, so the enclosing scope closes only after the body returns. The body must poll a {{c2::std::stop_token}}; otherwise the destructor blocks {{c3::forever}}.<pre><code>#include &lt;chrono&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;stop_token&gt;
#include &lt;thread&gt;
int main() {
    {
        std::jthread w([](std::stop_token st) {
            while (!st.stop_requested()) std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(1));
            std::cout &lt;&lt; "worker saw stop\n";
        });
        std::cout &lt;&lt; "scope ending\n";
    }
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "after scope\n";
}</code></pre><br><b>Real output:</b><pre><code>scope ending
worker saw stop
after scope</code></pre>
